12:["$","section",null,{"className":"reference-section battle-flow-section","id":"battle-flow","children":["$","$L19",null,{"guide":{"date":"April 3–6, 1862; ending with the opening shots at about 5 a.m. on April 6","place":"The roads from Corinth, Mississippi, to the United States camps around Shiloh Church and Pittsburg Landing, Tennessee","result":"Rain, mud, poor staff work, and inexperienced troops delayed the Confederate approach for two days. Beauregard and Bragg argued for canceling the attack, but Johnston ordered it to proceed. Grant and Sherman received repeated reports of nearby Confederates but did not expect a general attack or fortify the camps. Everett Peabody sent a 400-man patrol before dawn on April 6; it met Aaron Hardcastle’s Mississippi battalion in Fraley Field and opened the Battle of Shiloh","facts":[{"label":"Approach distance","value":"About 23 road miles from Corinth to the United States camps"},{"label":"Confederates too sick to march","value":"More than 7,600 remained near Corinth"},{"label":"Confederates reaching attack positions","value":"About 40,000 by the evening of April 5"},{"label":"Combat experience","value":"About 85 percent of Johnston’s soldiers had never been under fire"},{"label":"United States main camps","value":"Five divisions near Pittsburg Landing; no field fortifications"},{"label":"Confederate distance on April 5","value":"The first line deployed within about one mile of the camps"},{"label":"Peabody patrol","value":"About 400 men from the 25th Missouri and 12th Michigan"},{"label":"Battle opened","value":"About 5 a.m. Sunday, April 6, in Fraley Field"}],"maps":[{"title":"Corinth to Pittsburg Landing","caption":"The Confederate army used the Ridge and Monterey roads from Corinth. The routes converged near Mickey’s farm, about five miles south of Shiloh Church.","source":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright","embed":"https://www.openstreetmap.org/export/embed.html?bbox=-88.75%2C34.80%2C-88.15%2C35.30&layer=mapnik&marker=35.1488%2C-88.3228"},{"title":"The strategic approach","caption":"Johnston moved north from Corinth while Buell approached Savannah and Grant remained on the west bank of the Tennessee River.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-campaign-approach.svg","alt":"Strategic map of the Confederate and United States approaches to Shiloh"},{"title":"Confederate deployment and the first contact","caption":"Hardee, Bragg, Polk, and Breckinridge deployed south of the camps. At 3 a.m., Peabody sent Powell’s patrol forward. The patrol crossed the Corinth Road and met Hardcastle’s Mississippians in Fraley Field about two hours later.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=I","image":"/images/shiloh-opening-contact-map.svg","alt":"Map of the Confederate lines and Peabody patrol opening the Battle of Shiloh in Fraley Field"},{"title":"Shiloh battlefield and camp area","caption":"The modern NPS map shows the roads, fields, creeks, and river that shaped the camps and the Confederate approach.","source":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm","image":"/images/shiloh-nps-auto-tour-map.jpg","alt":"National Park Service map of the Shiloh battlefield"},{"title":"Historical troop-position map","caption":"This 1862 map approved by Beauregard shows the battlefield positions, Pittsburg Landing, Shiloh Church, camps, roads, and gunboats.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/","image":"/images/shiloh-beauregard-map.jpg","alt":"1862 manuscript map of Shiloh showing troop positions and battlefield landmarks"}],"moments":[{"time":"3:41","audioTime":221,"title":"The armies were twenty-three miles apart","detail":"Grant’s army camped around Pittsburg Landing while Johnston and Beauregard assembled the Confederate army at Corinth."},{"time":"4:44","audioTime":284,"title":"Johnston’s intended attack emphasized the river flank","detail":"The strongest push was to drive the United States left away from Pittsburg Landing and trap the army against Owl and Snake creeks."},{"time":"5:25","audioTime":325,"title":"The written order stacked the corps","detail":"Hardee formed the first line, with Bragg, Polk, and Breckinridge following across the same frontage."},{"time":"7:24","audioTime":444,"title":"The Confederate approach began","detail":"The army had to move twenty-three miles from Corinth to its deployment area near the United States camps."},{"time":"8:01","audioTime":481,"title":"Rain and poor staff work disrupted the march","detail":"Units used the wrong roads, blocked one another, and struggled to move artillery and wagons through deep mud."},{"time":"9:00","audioTime":540,"title":"More than 7,600 sick troops remained behind","detail":"Disease in the camps near Corinth reduced the force before the march began."},{"time":"10:35","audioTime":635,"title":"Two roads carried the army north","detail":"The Ridge and Monterey roads connected through smaller lanes and converged about five miles from Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"11:46","audioTime":706,"title":"Most of the army reached position","detail":"By the evening of April 5, about 40,000 Confederates were prepared to attack the next morning."},{"time":"11:59","audioTime":719,"title":"Beauregard proposed canceling the attack","detail":"He believed the army lacked food and that two days of skirmishing had warned Grant."},{"time":"12:39","audioTime":759,"title":"The commanders debated withdrawal","detail":"Beauregard, Bragg, Polk, Breckinridge, and Johnston met on Saturday evening. Bragg supported cancellation; Polk and Johnston supported attack."},{"time":"13:38","audioTime":818,"title":"Johnston ordered the attack to proceed","detail":"Reports indicated that the United States camps had not prepared for a general attack, so Johnston directed the assault to begin at daylight."},{"time":"14:24","audioTime":864,"title":"The Confederates bivouacked without fires","detail":"The troops slept close to the enemy in cold conditions, and many had little food or rest."},{"time":"16:33","audioTime":993,"title":"Grant did not expect an attack","detail":"He believed the Confederate concentration at Corinth was defensive and remained at Savannah, nine miles downriver."},{"time":"17:33","audioTime":1053,"title":"United States reconnaissance remained limited","detail":"Halleck’s order to avoid a general engagement contributed to shallow patrols and the dismissal of skirmishing as routine activity."},{"time":"18:10","audioTime":1090,"title":"The camps had no field fortifications","detail":"Grant, Sherman, and C.F. Smith treated Pittsburg Landing as a base for an offensive rather than a position likely to be attacked."},{"time":"20:38","audioTime":1238,"title":"The camp area could become a trap","detail":"A successful Confederate drive away from the river could pin Grant’s army against the creeks and cut it off from Pittsburg Landing."},{"time":"21:28","audioTime":1288,"title":"Sherman and Grant discounted the danger","detail":"Both sent messages on April 5 saying that they did not expect a major attack."},{"time":"22:42","audioTime":1362,"title":"The Confederate lines moved close to the camps","detail":"Hardee deployed first, Bragg formed about 1,000 yards behind him, Polk followed, and Breckinridge came into reserve."},{"time":"24:19","audioTime":1459,"title":"Reports increased along the picket line","detail":"United States soldiers in the forward camps saw Confederate cavalry, skirmishers, and other signs of a large force nearby."},{"time":"24:53","audioTime":1493,"title":"Jesse Appler alerted Sherman","detail":"The 53rd Ohio formed in Ray Field after reports of a strong skirmish line. Sherman rejected the warning and ordered the regiment back to camp."},{"time":"25:42","audioTime":1542,"title":"Prentiss reinforced his pickets","detail":"After seeing Confederate cavalry, he sent four companies of the 16th Wisconsin forward for the night."},{"time":"26:41","audioTime":1601,"title":"Peabody ordered a reconnaissance","detail":"At about midnight, Major James Powell reported hearing many troops. Peabody sent five companies, about 400 men, forward at 3 a.m."},{"time":"28:48","audioTime":1728,"title":"Powell entered Fraley Field","detail":"The patrol passed the 57th Ohio pickets, crossed the Corinth Road, and exchanged shots with Confederate cavalry before reaching the far fence line."},{"time":"29:46","audioTime":1786,"title":"Hardcastle’s battalion fired a volley","detail":"Mississippi infantry opened fire from the edge of Fraley Field. Powell’s men returned fire at about 5 a.m."},{"time":"30:37","audioTime":1837,"title":"Johnston ended the debate","detail":"Hearing the firing, Johnston stated that the battle had opened and ordered the army forward."}],"command":[{"side":"United States","color":"#315f8c","branches":[{"commander":"Henry W. Halleck","command":"Department of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Ordered Grant not to bring on a general engagement before Buell arrived"},{"commander":"Ulysses S. Grant","command":"Army of the Tennessee","subordinates":"Headquarters at Savannah; did not expect a major Confederate attack"},{"commander":"William T. Sherman","command":"5th Division / senior officer in the camps","subordinates":"Forward camps around Shiloh Church; dismissed multiple warnings"},{"commander":"Benjamin M. Prentiss","command":"6th Division","subordinates":"Reinforced the picket line after seeing Confederate cavalry"},{"commander":"Everett Peabody","command":"1st Brigade, Prentiss’s division","subordinates":"Ordered Powell’s five-company reconnaissance without informing Prentiss"},{"commander":"James E. Powell","command":"Reconnaissance detachment","subordinates":"Three companies, 25th Missouri; two companies, 12th Michigan; about 400 men"}]},{"side":"Confederacy","color":"#9a3d32","branches":[{"commander":"Albert Sidney Johnston","command":"Army of the Mississippi","subordinates":"Rejected cancellation and ordered the dawn attack"},{"commander":"P.G.T. Beauregard","command":"Second in command","subordinates":"Favored withdrawal on April 5; supervised the stacked-corps deployment"},{"commander":"William J. Hardee","command":"First battle line","subordinates":"Corps deployed nearest the United States camps"},{"commander":"Braxton Bragg","command":"Second battle line","subordinates":"Corps about 1,000 yards behind Hardee"},{"commander":"Leonidas Polk","command":"Third battle line","subordinates":"Supported Johnston’s decision to attack"},{"commander":"John C. Breckinridge","command":"Reserve Corps","subordinates":"Three brigades following Polk"},{"commander":"Aaron B. Hardcastle","command":"Advance infantry battalion","subordinates":"Mississippi troops at the western edge of Fraley Field"}]}],"people":[{"name":"Albert Sidney Johnston","role":"Rejected Beauregard’s proposal to withdraw and ordered the attack to proceed","side":"Confederacy","image":"/images/albert-sidney-johnston.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670318/"},{"name":"P.G.T. Beauregard","role":"Argued that surprise was lost and recommended returning to Corinth","side":"Confederacy"},{"personId":"ulysses-s-grant","role":"Remained at Savannah and made no defensive preparations for a general attack"},{"name":"William T. Sherman","role":"Acted as camp commander and dismissed warnings from the forward divisions","side":"United States","image":"/images/william-t-sherman.jpg","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018670507/"},{"name":"Everett Peabody","role":"Ordered the patrol that found the Confederate army and gave the camps their first warning","side":"United States"},{"name":"James E. Powell","role":"Led Peabody’s 400-man patrol into Fraley Field","side":"United States"},{"name":"Jesse Appler","role":"Commanded the 53rd Ohio, reported nearby Confederates, and posted an extra picket despite Sherman’s response","side":"United States"},{"name":"Aaron B. Hardcastle","role":"Commanded the Mississippi battalion that met Powell’s patrol at about 5 a.m.","side":"Confederacy"}],"losses":[],"gallery":[{"image":"/images/pittsburg-landing-1862.jpg","alt":"Pittsburg Landing on the Tennessee River in 1862","caption":"Grant’s supply base and line of retreat were at Pittsburg Landing, east of the inland camps.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battlefield-april-9.jpg","alt":"The Shiloh battlefield photographed April 9, 1862","caption":"This photograph records the wooded battlefield three days after Powell’s patrol opened the fighting.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"},{"image":"/images/shiloh-battle-print.jpg","alt":"Historical print of the Battle of Shiloh","caption":"The print shows infantry and artillery fighting across the broken, wooded ground around Shiloh.","source":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2018757088/"}],"sources":[{"label":"Civil War Podcast episode 113","url":"https://civilwarpodcast.libsyn.com/113-shiloh-part-the-third"},{"label":"National Park Service: Battle of Shiloh","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/shiloh-history.htm"},{"label":"National Park Service: Prentiss’s 6th Division","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=I"},{"label":"National Park Service: Hardee’s 3rd Corps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/learn/historyculture/monument-detail.htm?id=O"},{"label":"National Park Service: Shiloh maps","url":"https://www.nps.gov/shil/planyourvisit/maps.htm"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield map","url":"https://www.loc.gov/item/85690890/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Pittsburg Landing","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2004660985/"},{"label":"Library of Congress: Shiloh battlefield, April 9","url":"https://www.loc.gov/pictures/item/2017646904/"}]}}]}]
